meta {
  name: Me
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/me
  auth: inherit
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("carries the freshly resolved permission set", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("permissions").that.is.an("array");
    expect(body).to.have.property("roles").that.is.an("array");
    expect(body).to.have.property("dataScope");
  });

  test("matches the shape login returns", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.all.keys(
      "id",
      "email",
      "name",
      "firstName",
      "lastName",
      "avatarUrl",
      "roles",
      "agencyId",
      "branchId",
      "permissions",
      "scope",
      "dataScope",
      "isPlatformAdmin",
      // PAC-69. Recomputed on every `/auth/me` rather than cached with the
      // permission set: it flips the moment an owner finishes their agency's
      // first-run setup, and the client reads `me` far more often than a cache
      // TTL would turn over.
      "agencySetupPending",
      // `null` on a real login; the operator's id on an impersonated session
      // (PAC-70). Always present so the web's stored blob never keeps a stale
      // value after switching back to an ordinary login.
      "impersonatedBy",
    );
    expect(res.getBody().impersonatedBy).to.equal(null);
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /auth/me — the caller's identity and current permissions

  **Module:** none (`@SkipTenant`, `@SkipBranch`, `@SkipModule`).
  **Permission:** none beyond a valid access token.

  Returns exactly the `user` object that `POST /auth/login` returns, resolved
  from the database at request time. Both are built by one private
  `toAuthUser` helper so they cannot drift — the whole value of this endpoint is
  that a client can refresh the object and get the same shape, only current.

  The tenant and branch guards are skipped because a platform admin has neither
  an agency nor a branch, and "who am I" has to answer for them too.

  ## Why it exists

  The web keeps this blob in `localStorage` and reads permissions from it, but
  only rewrote it at login, token refresh and accept-invite. So an owner editing
  someone's permissions did not reach that person's browser until their access
  token refreshed — up to 15 minutes of a UI showing access the API had already
  revoked. The server side was never wrong: permissions are resolved from Mongo
  on every request and the JWT carries only
  `{ sub, agencyId, branchId, scope, isPlatformAdmin }`. This endpoint lets the
  client catch up.

  ## Response

  ```json
  {
    "id": "…",
    "email": "owner@demoagency.local",
    "name": "Agency Owner",
    "firstName": "Agency",
    "lastName": "Owner",
    "avatarUrl": "/me/avatar?v=1a2b3c4d",
    "roles": ["Agency Owner"],
    "agencyId": "…",
    "branchId": null,
    "permissions": ["agency:roles:read", "leads:read", "…"],
    "scope": "agency",
    "dataScope": "agency",
    "isPlatformAdmin": false,
    "impersonatedBy": null
  }
  ```

  `firstName`/`lastName` are the raw halves behind `name` (PAC-81), for the
  profile form. `avatarUrl` is null until a photo is committed; when set it is
  a **relative, authenticated** path (`GET /me/avatar`) carrying a
  key-derived cache-buster — fetch it with the bearer header, never from a
  bare `<img src>`.

  `roles` are display names — never branch on them. `permissions` is the
  authority, and `dataScope` is the widest across the roles the user holds.

  ## Errors
  - `401 Unauthorized` — no token, an expired one, or the user has since been
    deactivated. `AccessResolverService` returns null for an inactive user on
    every request, so a still-valid JWT stops working immediately rather than at
    expiry.
}
